  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{x}^{2}]}^{\u2032}}{[1-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(x)]^{\u2032}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x}{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x}{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[2x]}^{\u2032}}{{[\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(x)]}^{\u2032}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(x)} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(x)} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
